Build **Carom**, a neon top-down paddle duel that runs entirely in the browser.

Your project root is `{{workspace}}`. Build the game there.

## Your starting point

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
A TypeScript project is already in place at `{{workspace}}`, and its `README.md`
describes it. Its dependencies are installed, so you can build and test right
away: Vite builds it, Vitest tests it, ESLint lints it, and Prettier formats it.
That toolchain is the whole of what it supplies.

**What is missing is everything under `src/`, and that directory does not exist
yet.** Nothing here gives you a frame loop, a canvas fit, input, audio, an
overlay, or a line of game code. `index.html` loads `/src/main.ts`, so that
module is where your build starts: create it, and organize the rest of `src/`
however suits you.

The project's configuration **must not be edited**: `index.html`,
`tsconfig.json`, `vite.config.ts`, `vitest.config.ts`, `eslint.config.js`,
`.prettierrc.json`, `.prettierignore`, and `.gitignore`. `package.json` keeps its
existing entries and scripts, and takes one kind of change: a dependency you
genuinely need, added along with the updated `package-lock.json`.

The shape of the game's state is yours, under one contract: the `window.__carom`
surface `specs/instrumentation.md` specifies reads and poses that state, and you
write that surface too. It is a required deliverable, and a build that does not
install it, or installs one that departs from the specification, fails every
point whose check reaches the game through it.

Every number, color, key binding, cue name, and piece of screen copy the
specification fixes is stated in `specs/` under a name. Name each one once in a
module of your own and read it from there, rather than restating a value at each
use.
{{else}}
A TypeScript project is already in place at `{{workspace}}`, and its `README.md`
describes it. Its dependencies are installed, so you can build and test right
away. It is wired up end to end: Vite builds it, Vitest tests it, ESLint lints it,
Prettier formats it, and `src/main.ts` already stands the engine up over the
