**What is missing is the game itself: `src/game.ts`.** It exports `game`, a
`Game<CaromState, CaromDebugApi>` whose `initialize`, `update`, and `render` all
throw `"not implemented"`. Implement those three functions, splitting the work
across new modules under `src/` however suits you.

The case's own files and the project's configuration **must not be edited**:
`src/main.ts` (the entry point), `src/constants.ts` (every figure the
specification fixes), `index.html`, `.tcab/` (the vendored engine), `engine/`
(the engine's documentation), `tsconfig.json`, `vite.config.ts`, `vitest.config.ts`,
`eslint.config.js`, `.prettierrc.json`, `.prettierignore`, and `.gitignore`.
`package.json` keeps its existing entries and scripts, and takes one kind of
change: a dependency you genuinely need, added along with the updated
`package-lock.json`.

`src/game.ts` also declares and exports `CaromState` and the types it is built
from. `specs/state.md` states that declaration in full and it is a contract:
write it in as it stands, keep every field under its declared name, type, and
meaning, and hold no game state anywhere else. The seeded project does not
type-check until you have declared it, because the stub is written against that
type. That first `tsc` failure is expected rather than a broken seed, and
declaring the type clears it.

`src/game.ts` likewise declares and exports `CaromDebugApi`, the debug and
automation surface `specs/instrumentation.md` specifies, and you write that
surface too. `initialize` returns it beside the state it has just built, as
`[state, debug]`, and the engine returns that same value from `engine.debug`,
which is the one way the surface is reached. It is a required deliverable, and a
build that does not return it, or returns one that departs from the
specification, fails every point whose check reaches the game through it.

`src/constants.ts` is the single source of every number, color, key binding, cue
name, and piece of screen copy the specification fixes — import them, and never
restate one as a literal.
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
## The runtime

This build runs on no engine, so the layer beneath the game is yours as well:
the frame loop and the delta time it hands each update, fitting the fixed logical
field onto the canvas, the input actions the player drives the game with, the
audio, and the on-screen debug overlay. The specification in `specs/` states what
Carom needs from that layer and fixes nothing else about it, so how you structure
it is your call.

Keep it distinct from the game above it. The game advances from the elapsed time
it is handed and reads nothing from the renderer, which is what lets a scenario
posed through `window.__carom` replay identically.
{{else}}
## The {{engine.name}} engine

This build runs on the **{{engine.name}}** engine, installed as a dependency of
the project, so import it by its bare package name; there is nothing to add to
`package.json` for it and nothing to install.

The engine's own documentation is at `{{engine.docs}}`. Read all of it before you
start. It is the authoritative description of the engine's API — the specification
in `specs/` describes the game and does not restate the engine — so work from
those pages rather than from assumptions about how such a runtime usually behaves.
They also carry a complete worked example of unit-testing a game built on it,
which is the shape your own tests should take.

Build the game **on** the engine rather than beside it. The engine owns the frame
loop and the delta time it hands your update, the input actions the player drives
the game with, the audio, asset loading, and the on-screen debug overlay: do not
write your own frame loop, read keyboard events yourself, drive the Web Audio API
yourself, or draw the overlay yourself. Register what the game needs with the
engine and use what it gives back. What remains yours is the game itself — its
simulation and everything it draws.
{{/if}}

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What is checked

Deliver a complete, polished, playable game, not a tech demo. The hard
requirements — real graphics drawn in code, no backend or credentials, the palette
and type, the game states, a `README.md`, and a state that
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}`window.__carom`{{else}}the debug surface{{/if}}
(`specs/instrumentation.md`) can pose and read — are stated in full in
`specs/overview.md` and the files it points to. That specification is
authoritative; build to it rather than to a summary here. Four things bear
restating, because they are checked mechanically after the run:

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
- **Your code must type-check.** `npx tsc --noEmit` is run over the repository you
  leave behind, and code that does not compile scores zero.
{{else}}
- **Your code must type-check.** `npx tsc --noEmit` is run over the repository you
  leave behind, and code that does not compile scores zero. The project as seeded
  does not compile until `src/game.ts` declares `CaromState` and
  `CaromDebugApi`, so expect that failure on a fresh checkout and clear it with
  the declarations.
{{/if}}
- **Your code must lint and be formatted.** `npx eslint .` and
  `npx prettier --check .` are run over it too, and both results are recorded.
- **Write your own unit tests**, beside your sources as `src/**/*.test.ts`.
  `npx vitest run --coverage` is run over the repository and its test count and
  coverage are recorded. Cover the collision resolution, the paddle bounce and
  spin, the serve, the scoring and match rules, and the AI.
- **The build interface is fixed.** `npm ci` then `npm run build` must produce the
  static site — with no other manual step — into `dist/` at the project root, with
  an `index.html` at the root of that directory. Serving that directory as-is at
  the root of any static file server must run the game; it is deployed to static
  hosting exactly that way. The scripts that do this are already in
  `package.json`. Add a dependency only if you genuinely need one, and run
  `npm install` afterward so the committed `package-lock.json` matches.

`npm run typecheck`, `npm run lint`, `npm run format`, `npm test`, and
`npm run build` run the same commands locally. Leave the repository passing all
five.

## Driving the game in a browser

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The project's **Playwright** and its headless **Chromium** are installed, so you
can serve the built game and drive it in a real browser to watch a rally, check
for console errors, or exercise `window.__carom`. Whether and how far to do so is
your call; your unit tests are the faster loop.
{{else}}
The project's **Playwright** and its headless **Chromium** are installed, so you
can serve the built game and drive it in a real browser to watch a rally and
check for console errors. Whether and how far to do so is your call; your unit
tests are the faster loop.
{{/if}}

Whenever you do launch the browser, use the project's own Playwright: import it
from your project (for example `import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the
project-local CLI with `npx playwright`. There is **no** global Playwright on
`PATH` — it lives in your project's `node_modules`, like any other dependency.
Launch Chromium with the `--no-sandbox` argument — this container has no Chromium
sandbox, so a default launch will fail.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a **git repository** — it was initialized before you
started, with the starter files in its first commit. Your finished
implementation is published directly from this repository's commits, so work you
leave uncommitted is **not** published. When the build is complete and verified,
commit everything: run `git add -A` then `git commit`, and confirm `git status`
reports a clean working tree with nothing uncommitted or untracked. A
`.gitignore` already excludes `node_modules/`, the build output, and coverage
reports, so a clean tree does not mean committing those — but it does mean
committing your source, your tests, your `package-lock.json`, and your
`README.md`.
